@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] crates-load-only-exports
Game crates MUST load only exported files (each asset's `.glb` and its declared outputs), never an asset's
`src/` or `asset.toml`, and MUST NOT depend on how an export was made. They MUST NOT name a region or
depend on how the world is split into regions: the world is tiles and an index.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] one-feature-per-crate
Each crate MUST be one feature, published as one Bevy plugin. That plugin MAY add private plugins for the
feature's parts; no other crate adds them. What several screens share lives in `screens`. A crate
MAY use another crate only for what that crate publishes for others (as the viewer uses `characters`),
never for its internals.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] stable-under-temporal-anti-aliasing
The world is drawn with temporal anti-aliasing, so what is drawn MUST NOT change from frame to frame in screen space
unless it moves in the world: no screen-space dither, stochastic transparency or crossfading visibility range (the
world crate makes any range abrupt and logs an error). Detail MUST swap whole primitives, as a tile's versions
swap whole. A vertex shader that moves geometry MUST write motion vectors that say how it moved, or say on
purpose that it stood still, as the wind's do. It MUST also draw the main pass no further than its prepass did (the
wind's `DEPTH_MARGIN`): the two passes are compiled apart, and a vertex a rounding error further fails the depth test
and leaves a hole showing the clear colour for a frame.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] nothing-appears-after-loading
The world MUST NOT show until all it draws is ready: its tiles, models and textures loaded and every pipeline made,
whichever way the camera looks. What loads with the world MUST hold the splash until it is ready (the world's
`Unready`, or a load the splash checks), and MUST be spawned, if hidden, so that it is drawn under it, as the
paraglider is, too small to see. The game logs any pipeline made once the world has loaded. A file that fails to
load MUST be reported as an error and named on the splash, never counted as ready in silence. What the world can show
without (a texture, a tile, a plant, the paraglider) MUST then stop holding the splash; what it cannot (its index, the
player's character) keeps it up, saying which file failed.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] cut-outs-light-only-what-stays
A material that cuts out texels (alpha mask) MUST discard them before any lighting, as the plants' fragment shader
`leaves.wesl` does: in the browser a discarded fragment runs on to the end of the shader, so Bevy's standard shader
pays the full lighting and shadow lookups for every texel it throws away. Anything cut out MUST be drawn with that
shader or one that branches the same way. The world logs an error for a standard material that cuts out; that error
MUST stay silent.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] detail-never-swaps-in-view
Near the camera, in the tiles shown in full, a model MUST show the same version wherever the camera stands, so
nothing changes as the player walks up to it. A cheaper version there MAY only cast the shadow, from the
`SHADOW_ONLY` render layer the sun draws and the camera does not. Versions MAY change only where a whole tile changes
its detail, in whole primitives.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] full-screen-passes-are-written-out
A full-screen pass MUST read only the texels it uses, and a kernel that does not change while the game runs MUST be
written into its shader as constants, leaving out the weights that are zero (as `painterly.rs` writes its taps). The
code that writes it MUST fail at once if the shader no longer has the place it writes into.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] the-browser-paces-sixty
In the browser the game MUST NOT run its own timer for 60 frames a second or more: it runs apart from the display's
refreshes and gives frames of one and two refreshes by turns. A cap of 60 or more leaves the pacing to the browser;
only a lower cap waits on a timer.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] held-things-follow-this-frames-pose
What a character holds MUST be placed, and its hands put on it, from the pose the animation wrote this frame: in
`PostUpdate` after `AnimationSystems` and before `TransformSystems::Propagate`, in the body's frame, never from last
frame's global transforms, so the hands and what they hold never part for a frame. Where and how each hand holds it
MUST come only from its export's `holder` node (the skeleton that held it, its handles and palms), scaled to the
character's size, never from offsets tuned in the game. A fist that misses its handle MUST be logged as an error,
and a model that does not say how it was held MUST be reported and stay put away, never hold up the game.
